.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1159 AM PDT Wed Sep 2 2026
(This afternoon through Thursday)

KMUX shows a band of light precipitation bisecting the Bay Area and
moving east. Still waiting on a bucket tip south of the Golden Gate
Bridge; nonetheless, it won`t amount to much as the band continues
to weaken. This pre-frontal rain has overperformed the official
forecast with Mt. Tam measuring 0.57" in the last 24 hours. The
overperformance is likely due to the struggles of guidance/models in
the shoulder seasons. The cold frontal passage will occur tomorrow
afternoon/evening from northwest to southeast, bringing renewed
chances for rainfall alongside well below normal temperatures and
cloudy conditions. This second round of rain also runs the risk of
overperformance; however, the forecast has trended wetter. Totals of
0.10"-0.50" for most of the North Bay and 0-0.10" south of the
Golden Gate Bridge are expected with accumulation south of the
Golden Gate Bridge primarily favoring the San Francisco Peninsula
Coast. Looking at the 90th percentile, totals get bumped up with up
to 1.00" possible in the higher terrain of the North Bay and up to
0.50" along the San Francisco Peninsula Coast. While rainfall is
expected to be beneficial, wet roadways will create slick driving
conditions and increase stopping distance. Wind Advisory criteria is
not expected to be met; however, gusts of 30-40 mph can be expected
through gaps/passes and down the Salinas Valley.

