.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1159 AM PDT Wed Sep 2 2026
(Friday through next Tuesday)

A cutoff low will spawn in the Pacific Northwest from remnant upper-
level troughing as ridging expands westward into the Desert
Southwest. The low is expected to fill through the weekend, giving
the ridge increasing influence over the forecast. As such, a
gradual warming and drying trend and the reformation of the marine
layer is expected over the weekend. These features will also be
enough to shunt moisture from Tropical Storm Marie south of the
California Bight, at least for now. Even with a cutoff low in
play, there is relatively good agreement in the forecast through
Monday. By Tuesday, the ECMWF and GFS begin to drastically diverge
on their solutions with Marie. The one to watch will be the ECMWF
as it has another cutoff low digging, absorbing, and bringing
what was Marie into Central California, which would be a low
probability outlier at the moment. It`s imperative to note that if
it reaches California:
Marie will no longer be tropical and the primary impact will be
rainfall.

